    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d425">

                <pb facs="0570" n="558" sameAs="frus:frus1943v03/pg558"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.01/2273: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Standley</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1943-08-02T15:00:00+03:00">August 2, 1943—3
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received August 3—11:07 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>999. Today on the occasion of the third anniversary of the formation of the
                    Moldavian Soviet Socialist Eepublic a long article was published in <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Pravda</hi> which closely resembles those published on
                    the anniversary of the entrance of the Baltic States in the Soviet Union (see my
                    916, July 22, 8 a.m.<anchor n="55" xml:id="d425-f1"/>). The alleged terrible
                    conditions in Bessarabia before its incorporation into the Moldavian Republic
                    are compared with the prosperity brought to the region by Soviet rule. Greetings
                    are addressed to Stalin and the Red army. The article ends with the words
                    “Moldavia was and will be Soviet.”</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Standley</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
